            "name": "`features/ciso.md` \u2014 168 untracked lines already wired into the public nav, no card owns it, and its \"deterministic, no-LLM\" security claims contradict three open gate cards",
            "description": "## Why this card exists\n\nOn 2026-11-19 11:13Z I inventoried every docs file the next VitePress build would publish. Five were unowned. Four already have cards (`2410a44c`, `68566d25`, `721dc223`, `54658755`). **This one had none** \u2014 verified: `list_tasks(board=\"IT team\", query=\"features/ciso.md\")` \u2192 0 results against a 23-card board.\n\nIt is not a quiet file. It is untracked (`?`), 168 lines, and **already referenced three times** in the working tree:\n\n- `.vitepress/config.mts` \u2014 nav entry **CISO** (staged +2, working tree +3)\n- `features/index.md` \u2014 new table row in the feature catalogue (+3)\n- `features/users-and-security.md` \u2014 new inbound link (+10)\n\n`.vitepress/config.mts` has **no `srcExclude`** (measured: only `ignoreDeadLinks: true` at line 10). Every `.md` under the docs root compiles. So this page is in the build whether or not it is approved \u2014 the same exposure as `transformation/ai-company.md` on `721dc223`, and the same reason the 12:00Z freeze must cover the **build**, not the git index.\n\n## The problem with the page itself\n\n`features/ciso.md` states, in its own words, that the CISO view is backed by a **deterministic engine** and that **\"No security decision is delegated to an LLM.\"**\n\nThree open cards on this board say the shipped behaviour does not currently match that promise:\n\n- `19883bdd` \u2014 the CISO gate is **unusable on this project**: 1058 control-plane false positives plus an unreproducible `critical` that blocks every diff.\n- `6c697929` \u2014 the QA gate can return **`allow` with the tests switched off** (`run_tests=False`), and its `paths` filter is defeated by `.codx`. A false green is reachable today.\n- `0f73b32b` (D4) \u2014 `security_audit` accepts `..` and reads **outside the project**: the gate is not jailed.\n\nDeterministic is not the same as correct. A page that describes the mechanism as trustworthy while three cards document it failing is the exact shape of documentation that a security reviewer will hold against us.\n\n## What to do (owner: `biblio` \u2014 chosen because the deliverable is a documentation truthfulness verdict with citations, not a code change; no code is to be modified)\n\n**READ-ONLY. The docs freeze is in force until the 12:00Z watchdog `3cb512b4` has reported. Do not touch the working tree, do not stage, do not \"fix\" a sentence.**\n\n1. Read `features/ciso.md` end to end.\n2. For each factual claim about behaviour \u2014 the deterministic engine, the no-LLM statement, findings/redaction, the policy tabs, anything about gating \u2014 classify it as **verified in code**, **contradicted by code**, or **unverifiable from this repo**, with `path:line` evidence from the platform repo (`api/`, `client/`) or the docs-repo gate config. Quote the command or the line; do not infer.\n3. Cross-check every claim against the three cards above. Where a claim is contradicted, say which card already proves it.\n4. Give one recommendation: **publish as-is**, **publish with an explicit limitation note**, or **exclude from the build** (`srcExclude`) \u2014 with the reasoning in one paragraph.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A verdict per claim, each with `path:line` provenance; \"looks right\" is not a verdict.\n- The recommendation names the exact mechanism (nav entry, `srcExclude` pattern, or nothing) if it is not \"publish as-is\".\n- No repository file created, modified, staged or deleted by this card. If a fix is needed, it comes back to me as a proposal and lands **after** the 12:00Z decision, on its own, as a separate card.\n- This is the same standard I applied to `68566d25`: cite, do not infer.\n\n## Why it is scheduled for 20 Nov 09:00Z, not now\n\nThe page cannot be unpublished before the freeze is lifted, and any edit now would invalidate the third measured baseline in two days. The verdict is cheap (read + cite). It costs nothing to have it ready before the next deploy decision.",
